From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller 99998: write the measurement to every copy of the leg

cntlr_affiliated_sta_metrics() returned on the first affiliated element it
matched. One client can be held under more than one AP-MLD - a leftover from an
earlier association is ordinary in this database - and mapc_sync_apmld() walks
all of them, deleting the client's rows by MLD address and re-inserting from
whichever copy it reaches last.

So the reading went into one copy and the sync persisted another.

Measured on hardware 2026-08-04, from the controller log and a sampler reading
the database every ten seconds:

	07:15:53  cntlr_affiliated_sta_metrics: leg 06:52:f0:f8:c9:28
	                     on 76:04:1c:81:bc:2e  rcpi 130
	07:16:01  E2TRACE: ULOZEN sta_mld ... links=3      (rebuild + sync)
	07:16:05  affiliated_sta.rcpi = 0

rcpi 130 is -45 dBm and matches what libwifi reported on that leg. The value was
correct, arrived, and was written - into a copy nobody read.

Keep walking. The inner break still stops scanning legs of one STA-MLD once the
address matched, but every other copy now gets the same reading.

This is the third form of one habit in this series: 99994 wrote the node the
frame named rather than the one the TLV named, 99995 let one writer overwrite
another, and here one of several copies was updated as though it were the only
one. Where a value has more than one home, updating one of them is not an
optimisation - it is a coin toss.
--- a/src/cntlr_map.c
+++ b/src/cntlr_map.c
@@ -2977,7 +2977,26 @@
 					    MAC2STR(as->macaddr),
 					    MAC2STR(as->bssid), as->rcpi,
 					    as->dl_rate, as->ul_rate);
-					return;
+
+					/* Do not stop here. The same client can
+					 * be held under more than one AP-MLD -
+					 * a leftover from an earlier association
+					 * is normal in this database - and
+					 * mapc_sync_apmld() walks every one of
+					 * them, deleting the client's rows by MLD
+					 * address and re-inserting from whichever
+					 * copy it reaches last.
+					 *
+					 * Measured 2026-08-04: the writer set
+					 * rcpi 130 at 07:15:53, the rebuild and
+					 * sync ran at 07:16:01, and the database
+					 * read 0 at 07:16:05 - the reading had
+					 * gone into a copy the sync overwrote.
+					 * Updating one copy is not enough when
+					 * the reader does not care which one it
+					 * takes.
+					 */
+					break;
 				}
 			}
 		}
